      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE AND IMPORTANCE",
          "content": "Perineurioma, a rare benign nerve sheath neoplasm occurring in either an intraneural or soft tissue form, has never been reported to arise in the central nervous system."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL PRESENTATION",
          "content": "We describe the clinical, radiological, and pathological features of a perineurioma arising in the choroid plexus of the third ventricle in a 65-year-old woman and causing obstructive hydrocephalus."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "The lesion, apparently unassociated with a nerve, was gross totally resected by frontal craniotomy using a left-sided transcallosal approach. Short-term follow-up showed no evidence of recurrence."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Perineurioma of the variety found in soft tissue may occur in the central nervous system, wherein it shows the typical light microscopic, immunohistochemical (epithelial membrane antigen- and Collagen IV-positive, S-100 protein-negative), and ultrastructural (pinocytotic vesicles, discontinuous basement membrane) features."
        }
      ]},
